Yes. There is already a UK robot-repair/service market, and the evidence points to it expanding as adoption broadens beyond automotive factories into logistics, food, agriculture, healthcare, facilities and other service sectors. I would treat this as a very important POW vertical.

The strongest current signal is not just forecasts. The UK launched a **£52m Robotics Adoption Programme running April 2026–March 2030**, explicitly covering industrial robots, autonomous plant and service robots, and skills development. The accompanying government programme says the UK has historically under-adopted robotics and is specifically trying to accelerate deployment. ([UK Research and Innovation][1]) IFR recorded about **2,500 new industrial robot installations in the UK in 2024**, after 3,800 in 2023; so there is already a substantial installed base being continuously added to even before the newer adoption push. ([IFR International Federation of Robotics][2])

What I think happens is that “robot repair” does **not** initially emerge as one clean trade analogous to plumber. It splits into a stack:

```text
robot servicing
├── preventive maintenance
├── breakdown diagnosis
├── mechanical repair
│   ├── reducer
│   ├── bearing
│   ├── motor
│   ├── brake
│   └── harness
├── electrical/electronic repair
│   ├── servo drive
│   ├── power supply
│   ├── cabinet
│   ├── controller
│   └── sensors
├── calibration
├── robot programming
├── PLC / safety PLC
├── fieldbus / industrial networking
├── vision/perception
├── firmware/software
├── battery/service consumables
└── safety inspection / recommissioning
```

That is why **mechatronics/automation technician** is closer to the emerging occupation than “electrician.”

### The market already exists

The OEMs are already selling exactly the recurring services we'd expect in a mature repair market. FANUC UK has dedicated robot service and spare-parts operations from Coventry; KUKA UK sells repairs, maintenance, refurbishment, used robots, spares and service contracts; ABB Robotics sells preventative maintenance, diagnostics, on-site/workshop repair, reconditioning and lifecycle services; Yaskawa offers on-site repairs, motor/gearbox/component repair, spares and reconditioning. ([Fanuc][3])

And importantly, there is already an **independent aftermarket** rather than everything being locked to OEMs. Global Robots services ABB, KUKA, FANUC, Yaskawa/Motoman, Kawasaki and Stäubli robots, including on-site UK repairs and spare parts. RoboTeam offers multi-brand breakdown repair, preventative maintenance and reconditioned robots. There are also smaller specialist firms such as Art Robotic and newer companies explicitly positioning themselves as OEM-agnostic field service for commercial and emerging robots. ([Global Robots][4])

The integration ecosystem is broader still. BARA/Automate UK currently lists audited integrators including **RMGroup UK, Olympus Technologies, ABB Robotics UK and Newtech Intelligent Automation** under its Robot Integrator Standard; the standard explicitly includes installation and after-sales service as part of the integrator role. ([Automate UK][5])

So I wouldn't frame the future as:

> robots appear → suddenly someone invents robot repair.

It is:

> industrial automation service market already exists → installed base broadens enormously → service/repair becomes more geographically distributed and multi-brand → new commercial/mobile/humanoid robots create a much larger long-tail aftermarket.

That latter transition is the interesting POW opportunity.

### UK training is already converging on this

The single most relevant apprenticeship I found is **Level 4 Automation and Controls Engineering Technician**. The occupational standard could almost have been written for this thesis. It explicitly covers installation, commissioning, servicing, maintenance and fault finding of **robots, PLCs, HMIs, servo drives, safety systems and industrial networks**. It includes robot programming, diagnostic tools, three-phase/AC/DC motor and control circuits, industrial networking and machinery safety. It is currently approved for starts. ([Skills England][6])

Below that there is a **Level 3 Mechatronics Maintenance Technician** route. Skills England describes the occupation as installation, testing, fault finding, rectification, modification and planned maintenance of complex automated equipment across electrical, electronic, mechanical, fluid-power and control-system disciplines. ([Occupational Maps][7]) There are also newer Level 3 Engineering Maintenance Technician pathways in electrical and dual electrical/mechanical disciplines. ([Skills England][8])

And the government is now deliberately filling the robotics-specific skills gap. In May 2026 it confirmed a **Robotics Adoption Skills Development Programme worth up to £2.5m**, specifically intended to create course content and training for people who need to **procure, integrate, operate and maintain robotics and autonomous systems**. ([UK Parliament][9])

So yes: I would expect substantially more explicitly named robotics maintenance/training products over the next few years.

OEM training already exists too. ABB's Milton Keynes training centre teaches mechanical and electrical maintenance; KUKA runs a UK College in Wednesbury and offers maintenance paths; Yaskawa has courses explicitly titled things like **Mechatronic Maintenance Robot**, including practical replacement of motors, gearboxes, batteries and wiring harnesses plus recalibration; FANUC has a UK Academy alongside its service operation. ([ABB Group][10])

That Yaskawa curriculum is revealing. Robot repair isn't some mysterious futuristic skill. A lot of it is:

```text
diagnose alarm
isolate machine safely
replace motor / reducer / battery / cable
reassemble
calibrate axes
restore backups
test
recommission
```

AI will make the **diagnostic/information component** much easier. The physical intervention remains scarce.

## Do you need to be an electrician?

**No blanket rule says “you must be an electrician to repair a robot.”**

The relevant HSE rule is **competence for the specific work**. HSE says anyone working on electrical equipment, machinery or installations must be competent, based on appropriate training, experience and reassessment. ([HSE][11])

That produces roughly this division:

| Work                               | Likely competence                                     |
| ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| Replace mechanical gearbox/reducer | mechanical/mechatronic                                |
| Replace robot joint/module         | OEM/mechatronic                                       |
| Replace encoder/motor              | mechatronic + electrical competence                   |
| Change controller battery          | OEM/mechatronic                                       |
| Robot calibration                  | robotics/OEM                                          |
| Programming                        | robotics/controls                                     |
| Vision fault                       | controls/vision/software                              |
| PLC/network fault                  | automation/control                                    |
| Safety PLC/interlock changes       | specialist functional-safety competence               |
| Cabinet/mains/3-phase work         | industrial electrical competence                      |
| New fixed electrical installation  | appropriate electrical design/install/test competence |

The Level 4 Automation & Controls apprenticeship itself illustrates this beautifully: it is **not an electrician apprenticeship**, yet it explicitly requires understanding single- and three-phase supplies, electrical circuits, robots, servo drives and safety systems. ([Skills England][6])

Also, domestic **Part P** rules are mostly irrelevant to factory robots. Part P is concerned with electrical installations in dwellings and certain domestic-associated premises; an industrial robot cell in a normal separately metered factory is a different context. ([GOV.UK][12])

But robot repair can absolutely become dangerous electrical work. HSE requires machinery to be safely isolated during maintenance, including electrical power and stored hydraulic/pneumatic energy, with lock-off where necessary. ([HSE][13])

And this is likely to matter more, not less: HSE launched a new project in June 2026 specifically to create UK guidance for safe workplace use of **collaborative robots**, alongside Automate UK and the Manufacturing Technology Centre. ([HSE Media Centre][14])

## What this means for POW

This actually strengthens the architecture we just designed.

The future opportunity isn't merely:

```text
robot broken
→ find repairer
```

It's:

```text
ROBOT
  ↓
model / revision
  ↓
subsystem
  ↓
joint / actuator / controller / sensor
  ↓
failure mode
  ↓
required part
  ↓
part availability + price
  ↓
required intervention
  ↓
required competencies
  ↓
required OEM training
  ↓
required electrical competence
  ↓
required safety competence
  ↓
local qualified technician
  ↓
downtime
  ↓
repair outcome
```

Then we connect UK training:

```text
failure/intervention
       ↓ requires
competency
       ↓ gained_by
qualification/course
       ↓ delivered_by
training provider
       ↓ produces
local technician capacity
```

That means **`powuk` should absolutely start collecting this now**.

Specifically, the UK skills garden should track one thing we haven't emphasized enough: **robotics-maintenance capability supply**.

* Skills England occupational standards/apprenticeships
* apprenticeship vacancies by employer/location
* training providers offering those standards
* OEM training from FANUC/KUKA/ABB/Yaskawa
* BARA integrators and locations/capabilities
* independent robot service firms
* advertised field-service/automation/robotics engineer jobs
* required certifications and vendor experience
* skills mentioned: FANUC/KUKA/ABB/Yaskawa, PLC, Siemens, Rockwell, PROFINET, EtherCAT, servo, electrical, mechanical, safety
* course introduction/discontinuation through time
* eventually technician availability and callout geography

Because then a very POW-like graph emerges:

```text
robot installed base ↑
        ↓
maintenance demand ↑
        ↓
robot-specific skill demand ↑
        ↓
qualified technician supply lags
        ↓
callout rates / wages / downtime ↑
        ↓
training + apprenticeships respond
        ↓
capacity expands
```

That's literally the **Seesaw** again.

And the UK is unusually interesting because the government itself acknowledges that UK robotics adoption has historically lagged peers and is now spending specifically to push adoption and skills. ([Innovation Funding Service][15])

So for our purposes, **robot maintenance/repair should become one of the central things `powproducts + repair + powuk` are designed to measure**. Not because we need to start a robot repair company tomorrow, but because the data garden can watch an entirely new skilled trade/service economy form in real time.

